and joining us from pittsburgh to  discuss the issues in more detail is dr  amish adelga a senior scholar at the  johns hopkins center for health security  at the bloomberg school of public health  dr adelga let's start there  so much hope with the new year with the  vaccine but this rollout seems so slow  right  now why there's multiple reasons that  that are holding up these vaccines  turning into vaccinations  one is is that delivered doses isn't the  same thing as an administered dose and  when states get these  doses there is a process before they get  them to their  hospitals get them to their nursing  homes and they have to figure out to  make sure that the priority is right  that the right health care workers are  getting it  and there's a a staffing issue because  you have to remember that many of these  hospitals are dealing with the same  patients  with covet that they now need to get  pull staff from and then  create vaccination programs and that's  not easy to do and the other thing is is  that states sometimes have  unpredictability of when the doses are  going to arrive sometimes doses may  arrive late  sometimes they may arrive ahead of  schedule that's why there needs to be  federal coordination federal leadership  because  this can't this hodgepodge network  really can't really deliver vaccine  vaccines at the pace that we need to put  this pandemic behind us  doctor some are predicting that tomorrow  is going to be the busiest  travel day since the pandemic began  hospitals already being pushed to  capacity i know you're heading  to the hospital right after this  interview how concerned are you right  now about january  i'm very concerned we know that there  has been a surge of cases starting out  even before thanksgiving then then  augmented by thanksgiving and christmas  and new year so we're going to continue  to see  cases rise we're already at record  hospitalizations  and hospitals really can't in a  sustainable manner go through day-to-day  worrying about supplies staffing icu  beds  that all is going to push hospitals  closer to the breaking point or crisis  standards of care where they can't  do their other operations we already  have hospitals that have started to stop  non-emergency surgeries  this type of approach isn't going to  work we've got to get some  handle on the number of cases and the  number of hospitalizations and to do  that i think we have to get the vaccine  accelerated  because that's really what's going to  give us breathing room in the hospitals  but  that's going to take some time so i do  think january is going to be one of the  more deadly months that we've seen  in this pandemic doctor on top of that  you also have this new variant that we  know is in at least three states how  concerned are you about that and how do  you think that that changes  the sort of containment strategies  the new variant it appears has the  ability to transmit more efficiently  that's what the laboratory studies are  showing  that's what the statistical modeling is  showing so that means that we have to  double down on what we're doing we have  to be better at washing our hands  wearing face coverings avoiding crowded  congregated places  and and that really also adds more  urgency to the vaccination program  because we have to stay ahead of this  virus that's constantly going to be  evolving it doesn't change anything  specifically that we do in tactics it  just means we've got to do it better  because if this virus strain starts to  take hold  you will start to see an acceleration of  transmission the way they saw in the  united kingdom and  you can't really allow that to happen in  this type of an atmosphere in the united  states where we have uncontrolled spread  in many communities around the world  around the country you mentioned the uk  and in the uk they've decided to delay  giving that second dose in order to  vaccinate more people with the first  it's been something that's been  discussed here where do you stand with  that  this is a hard decision to make because  we know that there is some protection  that's given after the first dose  but all of the data is based on a  two-dose regimen so that's where we're  most confident that the vaccine  will work the uk made a decision based  on their their own standards  i don't necessarily think it's the same  decision the united states can make it  was good to discuss it  but i do think that the two-dose regimen  is the surest way  to do this but you know i think things  may change depending upon  what the dynamics are and what data we  get on just what a one-dosed  regimen would do okay dr amishadalja  thank you very much as always
 

